   "type": "done",
   "answer": "Based on the lecture material provided, here is the distinction between spatial and temporal locality, along with the reasoning and citations.\n\n### Definitions and Distinction\n\n**Temporal Locality**\n*   **Definition:** A piece of data is quickly reused in the future.\n*   **Key Characteristic:** The same data item is accessed multiple times in a short period.\n*   **Example from Lecture:** In the code snippet `C[i] = A[i] * B[i] + D`, the variable `D` is reused in every iteration of the loop. This is cited as an example of temporal locality [S3, p. 24].\n\n**Spatial Locality**\n*   **Definition:** The neighbors of the current data access tend to be accessed in the future.\n*   **Key Characteristic:** Data items that are physically close to each other in memory (e.g., adjacent array elements) are likely to be accessed soon after one another.\n*   **Example from Lecture:** In the same code snippet, the arrays `A`, `B`, and `C` are used in sequence (e.g., `A[0]`, `A[1]`, etc.). The lecture notes that \"A\", \"B\", \"C\" are used in sequence, which illustrates spatial locality [S3, p. 24]. The lecture also explicitly lists \"array\" as a general example of spatial locality [S1, p. 22].\n\n### Reasoning and Mechanism\n\nThe lecture explains that these two types of locality are the \"hot features\" that make caches useful [S1, p. 22].\n\n1.  **Addressing the Memory Wall:** High-speed caches are used to capture these localities to address the \"memory wall\" (the performance gap between CPU speed and memory speed) [S2, p. 23].\n2.  **Cache Line Fetching:** The lecture illustrates how spatial locality is exploited by the hardware. When a single element is accessed, the CPU fetches an entire \"Cache Line\" (a block of adjacent memory) at once. The diagram on page 24 shows that fetching `A[0]` brings in `A[1]` through `A[7]` in one fetch. Since the program accesses `A[1]` next, it is already in the cache, reducing memory access latency [S4, p. 24].\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Feature | Temporal Locality | Spatial Locality |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Core Concept** | Reuse of the *same* data. | Access to *neighboring* data. |\n| **Lecture Example** | Variable `D` reused in every loop iteration [S3, p. 24]. | Array elements `A[0]`, `A[1]`, etc., accessed in sequence [S3, p. 24]. |\n| **Hardware Benefit** | Keeps frequently used data in fast cache. | Fetches a block of data (cache line) so neighbors are ready [S4, p. 24]. |\n\n**Citations:**\n*   [S1] Page 22: Defines Temporal and Spatial Locality.\n*   [S2] Page 23: States that localities are captured by caches to address the memory wall.\n*   [S3] Page 24: Provides the code example `C[i] = A[i] * B[i] + D` illustrating both types.\n*   [S4] Page 24: Illustrates the \"Cache Line\" concept related to spatial locality.",
